0,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,-100,3]],"expected":1},{"args":[[-2,1,-3,0,2,0]],"expected":0},{"args":[[6,11,82]],"expected":1},{"args":[[-14,99]],"expected":-1},{"args":[[79,-34,-6,20]],"expected":1},{"args":[[-20,-69,-75,-56,-13,-97,-57]],"expected":-1},{"args":[[-96,-93,-79]],"expected":-1},{"args":[[-3,-24]],"expected":1},{"args":[[-3,3]],"expected":-1},{"args":[[-12,-86]],"expected":1},{"args":[[88,63,-11]],"expected":-1}],"gen":"(r, big) => {\n    if (big) { const a = r.arr(100000, -100, 100).map(x => x === 0 ? 1 : x); if (r.bool(0.3)) a[r.int(0, a.length - 1)] = 0; return [a]; }\n    return [r.arr(r.int(1, 8), r.pick([-3, -100]), r.pick([3, 100]))];\n  }","hints":["Does the size of each value matter for the sign, or only whether it is positive, negative or zero?","A single zero decides everything. Without zeros, each negative factor flips the sign.","Scan once: return 0 the moment you see a zero, otherwise flip a sign variable for each negative value."],"explain":{"intuition":"<p>Multiplying by a positive number never changes the sign, multiplying by a negative number flips it, and multiplying by zero makes the result zero forever. So the magnitudes are irrelevant: you only need to know whether there is a zero and whether the count of negatives is odd.</p>\n<p>Actually multiplying is the trap. In JavaScript, a thousand factors of 100 give <code>Infinity</code>, and <code>Infinity * 0</code> is <code>NaN</code>, so the \"obvious\" answer is wrong exactly on the arrays that contain a zero. Python's big integers would survive but spend time and memory on a number with hundreds of thousands of digits.</p>","steps":["Set <code>sign = 1</code>.","For each value: if it is 0, return 0 right away.","If it is negative, set <code>sign = -sign</code>.","After the loop, return <code>sign</code>."],"pitfalls":["Computing the product. It overflows to <code>Infinity</code> in JavaScript and then <code>Infinity * 0</code> gives <code>NaN</code>.","Counting negatives but forgetting the zero check, so <code>[4, -1, 0, -6]</code> reports 1 instead of 0.","Returning the product clamped to a range, such as <code>Math.sign(product)</code>, which has the same overflow problem."],"complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"}},"solutions":[{"title":"Flip on every negative","idea":"Track only the sign: a zero ends it, each negative value flips it.","time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)","ts":"function productSign(nums: number[]): number {\n  let sign = 1;\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    if (x === 0) return 0;\n    if (x < 0) sign = -sign;\n  }\n  return sign;\n}","py":"class Solution:\n    def productSign(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        sign = 1\n        for x in nums:\n            if x == 0:\n                return 0\n            if x < 0:\n                sign = -sign\n        return sign","js":"function productSign(nums) {\n  let sign = 1;\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    if (x === 0) return 0;\n    if (x < 0) sign = -sign;\n  }\n  return sign;\n}"}],"followups":["Values arrive as a stream and can also be removed again. How do you keep the sign up to date in O(1) per change?","Instead of the sign, return the last three digits of the absolute product. Which arithmetic trick keeps the numbers small?"],"related":[],"kind":"function","nEx":3,"nBig":2,"lcTitle":"Sign of the Product of an Array","acc":0.006463876656216828,"co":[["Microsoft",28,0],["Meta",18,180],["Amazon",10,180],["Google",6,90]]}